[patterns/application/api-microservices/PTN-APP-API-024-event-driven-architecture-pattern.md#variation-1-event-notification-thin-events]

#### Variation 1: Event Notification (Thin Events)

**Scenario**: Minimize event size when events occur frequently and consumers need current state

**Implementation**:

```csharp
// Event contains minimal data - just IDs and notification
public record MemberUpdatedEvent
{
    public string EventId { get; init; }
    public string MemberId { get; init; }
    public DateTime UpdatedAt { get; init; }
    public string UpdateType { get; init; } // \"Demographics\", \"Eligibility\", \"Address\"
    public string CorrelationId { get; init; }
    // No actual member data - consumers query member API for current state
}

// Consumer queries for current state
public class MemberUpdatedConsumer : IConsumer<MemberUpdatedEvent>
{
    private readonly IMemberApiClient _memberApi;

    public async Task Consume(ConsumeContext<MemberUpdatedEvent> context)
    {
        var evt = context.Message;

        // Query member API for current state
        var member = await _memberApi.GetMemberAsync(evt.MemberId);

        // Update local projection/cache
        await UpdateLocalMemberCacheAsync(member);
    }
}
```

**When to Use**:
- Events occur very frequently (100K+ per second)
- Event payload would be large (member has extensive demographic data)
- Consumers need most current state, not historical values
- Multiple concurrent events may occur for same entity

**Trade-offs**:
- **Pros**: Smaller event size; consumers always get current state; avoids stale data issues
- **Cons**: Additional API call adds latency; consumer coupled to producer API; can't replay events to recreate historical state; API must be available

