#### Variation 2: Event-Carried State Transfer (Fat Events)

**Scenario**: Events carry complete state to eliminate consumer queries

**Implementation**:

```csharp
// Event contains complete member state
public record MemberUpdatedEvent
{
    public string EventId { get; init; }
    public string MemberId { get; init; }
    public DateTime UpdatedAt { get; init; }
    public string CorrelationId { get; init; }

    // Complete member data included in event
    public MemberData Member { get; init; }
}

public record MemberData
{
    public string MemberId { get; init; }
    public string FirstName { get; init; }
    public string LastName { get; init; }
    public DateTime DateOfBirth { get; init; }
    public Address Address { get; init; }
    public string SSN { get; init; }
    public EligibilityInfo Eligibility { get; init; }
    // ... complete member state
}

// Consumer uses event data directly
public class MemberUpdatedConsumer : IConsumer<MemberUpdatedEvent>
{
    public async Task Consume(ConsumeContext<MemberUpdatedEvent> context)
    {
        var member = context.Message.Member;

        // Use member data from event - no API call needed
        await UpdateLocalMemberProjectionAsync(member);
    }
}
```

**When to Use**:
- Consumers need complete context to process event
- Eliminating API calls improves performance and reliability
- Event replay needs to recreate historical state accurately
- Consumer services should work independently without calling producer APIs

**Trade-offs**:
- **Pros**: No additional API calls; consumers fully decoupled; better performance; can replay historical state
- **Cons**: Larger event size increases storage and bandwidth costs; duplicates data across events and APIs; schema evolution more complex

