#### Variation 4: Event Choreography vs Orchestration

**Event Choreography** (Distributed Logic):

```csharp
// Each service reacts to events independently - no central coordinator
// Claim workflow emerges from service interactions

// Claims Service publishes event
await _eventBus.Publish(new ClaimSubmitted { ClaimId = claimId, ... });

// Eligibility Service reacts
public class ClaimSubmittedConsumer : IConsumer<ClaimSubmitted>
{
    public async Task Consume(ConsumeContext<ClaimSubmitted> context)
    {
        var result = await CheckEligibilityAsync(context.Message);
        await context.Publish(new EligibilityChecked { ClaimId = ..., IsEligible = result });
    }
}

// Fraud Service reacts
public class ClaimSubmittedConsumer : IConsumer<ClaimSubmitted>
{
    public async Task Consume(ConsumeContext<ClaimSubmitted> context)
    {
        var result = await RunFraudDetectionAsync(context.Message);
        await context.Publish(new FraudCheckCompleted { ClaimId = ..., IsSuspicious = result });
    }
}

// Adjudication Service waits for eligibility + fraud, then processes
public class ClaimReadyForAdjudication : Saga<ClaimSagaState>
{
    // Waits for both EligibilityChecked AND FraudCheckCompleted events
    // Then publishes ClaimAdjudicated event
}
```

**Event Orchestration** (Central Coordinator):

```csharp
// Saga orchestrates workflow centrally - explicit control flow
public class ClaimProcessingSaga : MassTransitStateMachine<ClaimSagaState>
{
    public ClaimProcessingSaga()
    {
        InstanceState(x => x.CurrentState);

        Initially(
            When(ClaimSubmitted)
                .Then(context => {
                    context.Instance.ClaimId = context.Data.ClaimId;
                    context.Instance.SubmittedAt = DateTime.UtcNow;
                })
                .TransitionTo(ValidatingEligibility)
                .Publish(context => new CheckEligibility { ClaimId = context.Instance.ClaimId }));

        During(ValidatingEligibility,
            When(EligibilityChecked)
                .If(context => context.Data.IsEligible,
                    binder => binder
                        .TransitionTo(CheckingFraud)
                        .Publish(context => new RunFraudCheck { ClaimId = context.Instance.ClaimId }))
                .If(context => !context.Data.IsEligible,
                    binder => binder
                        .TransitionTo(Rejected)
                        .Publish(context => new ClaimRejected { ClaimId = context.Instance.ClaimId })));

        During(CheckingFraud,
            When(FraudCheckCompleted)
                .If(context => !context.Data.IsSuspicious,
                    binder => binder
                        .TransitionTo(Adjudicating)
                        .Publish(context => new AdjudicateClaim { ClaimId = context.Instance.ClaimId }))
                .If(context => context.Data.IsSuspicious,
                    binder => binder
                        .TransitionTo(Pending)
                        .Publish(context => new ClaimPendedForReview { ClaimId = context.Instance.ClaimId })));
    }
}
```

**When to Use Each**:
- **Choreography**: Simple workflows, high autonomy, services own their domain logic
- **Orchestration**: Complex workflows with conditional logic, need visibility into workflow state, regulatory audit requirements

**Trade-offs**:
- **Choreography Pros**: High decoupling, service autonomy, no single point of failure
- **Choreography Cons**: Workflow logic distributed across services, harder to understand end-to-end flow, difficult to add transaction compensation
- **Orchestration Pros**: Centralized workflow logic, easier to understand and modify, supports complex conditional flows, transaction compensation
- **Orchestration Cons**: Orchestrator is coupling point, orchestrator can become bottleneck, services less autonomous

